  "titleEn": "Hipsters",
  "titleEs": "Hipsters",
  "titleText": {
    "es": "Se podría afirmar que este mismo recurso al distanciamiento irónico, participando al mismo tiempo de la cosa en cuestión, es la definición misma de hipsterismo contemporáneo. Pero, por otro lado, un momento, estás en una habitación vacía. ¿A quién le hablas?",
    "en": "You may point out that this very retreat into ironic detachment while still clearly participating in the thing in question is the very definition of contemporary hipsterdom. But on the other hand, wait, you're in an empty room. Who are you talking to?",
    "noteEn": "The Spanish and official English title attributes are preserved exactly. OCR followed the graph curves rather than semantic source order, fused decorative strokes into spurious 1 and 4S tokens, and split the printed wrap PA-/LABRA; the authored inventory restores one PALABRA and audits only the two axis arrows as exclusions."
  },
  "cardDefinitions": [
    { "id": "word-frecuencia", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "frecuencia", "answerEn": "frequency; how often something happens", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "frequency", "noun", "A2"] },
    { "id": "word-hipsters", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "hipsters", "answerEn": "hipsters", "noteEn": "Hipster is a loanword used in Spanish for someone associated with deliberately alternative, fashionable, or non-mainstream tastes. The plural keeps the English-looking spelling hipsters.", "example": { "es": "Ese barrio atrae a muchos hipsters.", "en": "That neighborhood attracts many hipsters." }, "tags": ["word", "loanword", "subculture", "B1"] },
    { "id": "word-quejas", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "quejas", "answerEn": "complaints", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "opinion", "noun", "A2"] },
    { "id": "word-continuo--continual", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "continuo", "answerEn": "continual; repeated", "noteEn": "Continuo describes something that keeps recurring or carries on. This adjective is written without an accent; continuó with an accent would be a past verb form.", "example": { "es": "El continuo ruido resulta molesto.", "en": "The continual noise is annoying." }, "tags": ["word", "adjective", "frequency", "B1"] },
    { "id": "word-abuso", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "abuso", "answerEn": "overuse; abuse", "noteEn": "With a word or expression, abuso can mean using it excessively until it becomes tiresome.", "example": { "es": "Criticaron el abuso de esa expresión.", "en": "They criticized the overuse of that expression." }, "tags": ["word", "usage", "noun", "B1"] },
    { "id": "word-hipster", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "hipster", "answerEn": "hipster", "noteEn": "Hipster is an English loanword used in Spanish for a person linked to deliberately alternative tastes or style.", "example": { "es": "La revista describe un estilo hipster.", "en": "The magazine describes a hipster style." }, "tags": ["word", "loanword", "subculture", "B1"] },
    { "id": "word-de--queja-content", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "de", "answerEn": "that; about the fact that (after queja)", "noteEn": "After queja, de can introduce the content of the complaint. English often uses ‘that’ rather than translating de separately.", "example": { "es": "Hubo quejas de que faltaba información.", "en": "There were complaints that information was missing." }, "tags": ["word", "preposition", "complaint content", "B1"] },
    { "id": "word-todo--every-before-singular", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "todo", "answerEn": "every; any (before a singular noun)", "noteEn": "Before a singular noun without an article, todo can cover every instance of that kind. This differs from standalone todo, ‘everything.’", "example": { "es": "Todo nivel requiere práctica.", "en": "Every level requires practice." }, "tags": ["word", "determiner", "generality", "A2"] },
    { "id": "word-meta--prefix", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "meta", "answerEn": "meta-; about itself or a higher level of the same subject", "noteEn": "The prefix meta- forms words for something that comments on, describes, or operates at a level above the original thing. It may be shown separately before a hyphen when its parts are being emphasized.", "example": { "es": "La novela incluye un metacomentario sobre su propia historia.", "en": "The novel includes meta-commentary about its own story." }, "tags": ["word", "prefix", "meta", "B2"] },
    { "id": "word-ombliguismo", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "ombliguismo", "answerEn": "navel-gazing; self-absorbed introspection", "noteEn": "Ombligo means ‘navel.’ Ombliguismo figuratively criticizes excessive attention to oneself or one's own small circle.", "example": { "es": "El ensayo critica el ombliguismo del sector.", "en": "The essay criticizes the sector's navel-gazing." }, "tags": ["word", "figurative", "criticism", "C1"] },
    { "id": "word-petardo--annoying-adjective", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "petardo", "answerEn": "annoying; tiresome", "noteEn": "In informal Spanish, petardo can describe a person or thing that is a bore or a pain. As an adjective, its ending follows the noun it describes.", "example": { "es": "Fue un discurso bastante petardo.", "en": "It was a pretty tiresome speech." }, "tags": ["word", "colloquial", "adjective", "B2"] },
    { "id": "word-insegura", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "insegura", "answerEn": "insecure; lacking confidence", "noteEn": "Insegura is feminine singular. Spanish describing words agree with the grammatical gender and number of what they describe.", "example": { "es": "La gente insegura busca aprobación.", "en": "Insecure people seek approval." }, "tags": ["word", "adjective", "emotion", "B1"] },
    { "id": "word-empeoran", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "empeoran", "answerEn": "they make it worse; they worsen", "noteEn": "Empeoran is a plural present form of empeorar. With lo before it, lo stands for the situation being made worse.", "example": { "es": "Esos cambios lo empeoran.", "en": "Those changes make it worse." }, "tags": ["word", "verb", "change", "A2"] },
    { "id": "word-actualidad", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "actualidad", "answerEn": "the present day; current times", "noteEn": "Actualidad refers to what is current now. It does not mean the English word ‘actuality.’", "example": { "es": "El gráfico llega hasta la actualidad.", "en": "The graph reaches the present day." }, "tags": ["word", "time", "false friend", "A2"] },
    { "id": "phrase-uso-y-abuso-de", "kind": "phrase", "promptEs": "uso y abuso de algo", "questionEn": "How do you criticize both the use and excessive use of something?", "answerEn": "Use uso y abuso de followed by the thing being used.", "noteEn": "Uso is ordinary use, while abuso intensifies the idea to overuse or misuse. The two nouns form a conventional pairing, and de introduces their object.", "example": { "es": "El artículo analiza el uso y abuso de las estadísticas.", "en": "The article analyzes the use and abuse of statistics." }, "tags": ["phrase", "criticism", "usage", "B1"] },
    { "id": "concept-hipster-meta-complaint-frequency-graph", "kind": "concept", "promptEs": "hipsters · quejas · meta-opinión · gráfica", "questionEn": "What progression do the rising curves satirize?", "answerEn": "Each later curve represents a more self-aware complaint about hipsters, ending with a graph that complains about the whole chain and thereby adds yet another level.", "noteEn": "The chart moves from seeing hipsters, to complaints about them, to complaints about the repeated word, and then to complaints that all such meta-commentary is the same insecure navel-gazing. The final curve says that graphs make everything worse, but that graph is itself another example. The similarly rising curves visualize an escalating loop of ironic detachment.", "tags": ["concept", "meta-commentary", "graph", "joke"] }
  ],
